 simultaneously.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:37.540
 Let's break them down a little bit like you are a chef, and you have been for a long time. But it

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:43.100
 seems like you've interacted with a lot of other parts of the food system along that journey of

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.840
 cooking for people. So how'd you start cooking?

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:56.700
 So it goes back to a personal transformation story. I grew up thinking I wanted to teach and assuming that

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:05.090
 I'd be a teacher after I got out of college. And so I graduated from college and eventually made my

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:12.770
 way to teaching via waitressing in my 20s. And I spent a year teaching in East Oakland and was

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:18.370
 really miserable and sort of had this revelation, like, I actually don't want to be a teacher.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:22.530
 And then I was left with this big question. Well, what do I want to do? Because that was what I'd always

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:28.930
 assumed that I wanted to do. And it was during that time I took, I gave myself six months off.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:36.450
 And during that six month time period, I just gave myself the space to explore, you know, do some

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:43.840
 soul searching. I worked a lot with the book, The Artist's Way. I worked through the 12 weeks of that

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:50.400
 with a friend. And that was really transformative because it's designed to unearth things that are

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.260
 buried inside you that you don't, that your conscious mind isn't aware of that you might want

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:00.220
 to do or be drawn to or, um, and it's about creativity. But for me, it was very much about

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:05.370
 finding my life's path. And what I realized is that I'd always been really interested in food

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:13.730
 and had a prejudice via my, my sort of family culture that food wasn't important. And this was

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:20.000
 early to mid nineties. And really this revolution in the idea that food is really important hadn't

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:28.150
 happened yet. So it wasn't yet in the society, this idea that, that food is profoundly important on a

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:36.480
 political and economic and ecological level. So it, it was sort of me realizing that, that food was

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:44.230
 political, was ecological, was personal, and that it was very connected to health and to our personal

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:49.590
 health and to the health of the planet. And so through that realization, then I gave myself

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:54.710
 the space to pursue it as a career. And so by that time, by the time I figured that out, I was,

00:07:54.710 --> 00:08:00.310
 I had been using up savings. And so then I was broke. So then I had to work waitressing for another,

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.910
 I don't know, six months to a year or something like that, saved up money. And then I went to cooking

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:08.830
 school in New York to the natural gourmet, which at the time was the only chef's training program

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:12.710
 that was focused on food and health in the environment. And so that was always central to me. I never

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:17.750
 really wanted to be like a restaurant chef or a gourmet chef that was not of interest. It was always about

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:24.620
 healing foods, and ecological sustainability. And now there's many programs that offer that including

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:30.780
 local ones, but at the time there wasn't. So I went to New York. And then I came back. And I had had my

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:37.920
 revelation in at cooking school was that food was also very much about relationship and was about people. And

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.590
 I decided that I didn't want to be cooking like behind closed doors for people that I didn't.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:54.160
 Meet or see. So then that knocked out almost all chef jobs. But I threw a very fortuitous and

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:59.520
 synchronistic series of events ended up as the chef at the Headland Center for the Arts in Marin, which

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.560
 was really my dream job because I was cooking for people that I knew. And they also happened to be

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.960
 artists. And so they were very much embedded in a in a creative space for themselves. And so I got to feed their

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:19.040
 their creativity, their work and get paid to do it and be in a beautiful space. It's a famous kitchen designed by

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:28.460
 Ann Hamilton with an Alan Scott oven on one end brick oven wood fired oven. And it was just amazing space to cook in for

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:39.610
 four years. So I did that. But then by the end of that, I realized that I really I my dream job actually

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:47.680
 also had to include more sort of intellectual work around food. And so even though I loved cooking all

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:53.280
 the time, it's you know, it's physically very exhausting. I cooked from for 10 to 100 people every night, you know,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:10:02.030
 five nights a week and I needed to find, I knew that the next stage would include some form of teaching or

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:10.740
 sort of more explicitly political or intellectual work. So I finally left that job. And then I started

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:17.790
 doing catering for food systems, workshops and events. And so that was great, because I would,

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:23.230
 I tried to make the food match the conversation, which often doesn't happen, like people are having

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:29.150
 the conversation, but then what they're actually eating is not and that drives me crazy. I really

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:34.910
 can't stand those kinds of inconsistencies. And a lot of my life's work is about trying to

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:43.470
 make things, you know, make the walk match the talk, um, in some way or another. So, uh, I did that for a while.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:49.550
 And then that led to a job at the fairy plaza farmers market as the director of education for

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:55.390
 Queso, the center for urban education about sustainable agriculture. And so that was great because I was doing, um,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:11:02.430
 education work around food systems issues and also doing cooking classes as part of it, which I'd gotten more

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:10.670
 into wanting to, you know, empower other people to cook for themselves. And that was good, except,

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:15.470
 especially with the cooking classes, I noticed how many people just came to watch. And there's just like a huge

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:22.670
 culture of voyeurism around cooking and because of cooking television and all this stuff, you know, um, uh,

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:29.200
 food network, et cetera. And I was sort of shocked because I was trying to be really practical about it again,

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:36.740
 like in my earnest way, like, you know, I went to the market and I bought, like, I would walk around

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.860
 the market and I would buy these ingredients and then I would actually prep and cook them in front

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:45.340
 of everybody. I had, uh, um, a program I called real meals real fast because I was trying to make

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:48.940
 the connection between all these ingredients that you're surrounded by and dinner on the table.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.480
 Because I was really shocked how many people spark shop at the farmer's market and only

00:11:54.120 --> 00:12:01.640
 just buy a few things to eat, but don't like actually make that their food, their daily food.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.660
 And, and so I was trying to make that connection, but again, people just came to watch and a lot of

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:11.940
 people wouldn't really ever do it. And I started to realize that one of the challenges in getting

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:17.840
 people to cook at home has to do with the way that we've set up our society, which is very atomized.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:22.400
 People live in very small households for the most part. Some people live alone. A lot of people live

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:28.240
 alone. Um, people live with one partner, maybe one child. It's a household of one, two, three,

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:36.460
 maybe four individuals at the most. And cooking at that scale is, has some inherent problems with it.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:44.310
 It's not very efficient. It's not particularly economical. It's not particularly ecological and

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:51.870
 it's not particularly fun. And so I started, whereas, you know, traditionally when people were

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:57.560
 living in a culture where they're making all their food from scratch, they tended to be either living

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:05.590
 in a sort of extended family household, um, or in a village sort of atmosphere. And I had been cooking

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:10.950
 in the Headland Center for the Arts, which was again, 10 to a hundred people, which has a huge amount of,

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:20.070
 um, efficiencies of scale essentially. So, and I had, while I was at the Headland Center for the Arts,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:27.240
 also, I had been very, um, struck by how many people would go through the artist residency program

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:32.600
 would so appreciate this eating and community and having food prepared for them. And then at the end,

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:39.740
 they would be sort of bereft to go back to our society and have like, be like, I don't know what

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:47.350
 to eat. Like, I'm not a cook. I don't have time to cook. Um, and I can't eat McDonald's anymore

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:53.590
 because you've ruined it. And I don't know what I'm going to do. And that just really struck me that

00:13:53.590 --> 00:14:00.790
 we just don't have options. Um, along with this whole thing that I started thinking about scale.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:07.560
 So I started to frame it in terms of that our society sort of offers us two main different

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:13.000
 scales for food. And one is factory scale, which is really making us all sick and devastating the

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:20.520
 environment and, um, economically ruining everything as far as I'm concerned. And then the home scale,

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:26.840
 which, um, is great, but it's sort of, um, it's limited to it's, it's, it's constrained by all these

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:32.470
 factors that I was just mentioning and really feeling like we need to build something intermediate,

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:36.900
 you know, something that I consider community scale, you know, something that's larger than

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:42.820
 the home scale, but smaller than the factory scale. And so that's really how the CSK idea crystallized

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:50.290
 as trying to be sort of a communal and community-based solution to this problem of a lack of nourishing

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:55.410
 foods. Um, and especially as I got into the traditional diets work, you know, read nourishing

00:14:55.410 --> 00:15:02.360
 traditions and listen to Sally Fallon and, and all of that, uh, it, the problem only exacerbated because

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:07.880
 then it's not just cooking it from scratch, but it's also fermenting and it's making long simmered bone

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:14.690
 broth and soaking all your grains. And it just makes the cooking that much more labor intensive, um,

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:19.570
 and that much more difficult to do and making that much less sense if you're only doing it for one or two

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:29.820
 people. So, um, I sort of coalesced a group, um, of people around this idea who were all had similar and

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:36.060
 overlapping interests and, um, and we started meeting. And then from that point on, it was a

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:43.020
 group endeavor. Um, the five of us planned the, the, um, wrote the business plan, decided on the name,

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:50.090
 found the location incorporated as a cooperative, et cetera. And so for the past, and we're just

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:55.200
 celebrating 10 years right now. So for 10 years, we've been doing it. And three of the five co-founders

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:02.620
 are still in the company. And now we've got 20 worker owners and about 20 employees and, um, you

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:08.750
 know, anywhere from 500 to 800 orders every week that we're filling.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:15.920
 Wow. Well, there's a lot of questions for me about that. That was a lot of information. Um,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:23.850
 but I'm interested in kind of this middle space. I think I was a volunteer here about six years ago.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:29.240
 And, um, one of the things that I noticed about, well, there's a lot of things I noticed

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:34.280
 about working here that really stood out to me, but one of them was that, um, I was always really

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:40.200
 amazed at people's orders, like what they ordered and who these people were and how this food fit

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:45.280
 into their life. Right. So a lot of the things you're talking about are things like kombucha or

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:50.320
 bone broth or these very like labor intensive things that I hear all the time. People say like,

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:56.720
 I can't do that. Right. And I think there's lots of politics of gender and labor and time and

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:03.520
 how you do or don't care for your body and costs and all these things embedded in that. But when,

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:10.610
 but this felt like this was another grocery store in a certain way, this was another farmer's market.

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:15.790
 Like you, this is one of those stops you make in your week. And for lots of people,

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:19.230
 it wasn't the totality of all of their meals for the week. So maybe,

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:23.470
 can you talk about the community supported kitchen model for people who don't know what that is and

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:28.660
 what role that plays in people's lives? Sure. Yeah. So, um, the way that we work

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:33.860
 is we have an online menu. So we have a different menu each week. We have some things we make every

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:38.420
 week, like chicken bone broth and beef bone broth and, you know, a handful of things that are staples,

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:47.130
 but then most of the menu rotates or changes from week to week. And so we plan a menu for each week.

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:54.820
 That menu goes online for pre-order sales on Thursday evening. People have almost a week to

00:17:54.820 --> 00:18:00.020
 order from it. So from Thursday evening to the following Wednesday morning, the pre-ordering is

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:04.820
 open online. And so people have accounts and they go in and they say, I want one of this, two of that.

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:09.780
 Uh, we also have a range of retail items that we carry as well, in addition to what we make.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:17.400
 And then, um, we close the menu ordering system on Wednesday morning. Uh, we're printing out all the

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:26.340
 pre-orders and then on Wednesday evening and Thursday is our big pickup windows. So people come and pick up

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:31.860
 the orders that they pre-ordered. We also have delivery to Marin, San Francisco and the East Bay.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:38.980
 And so those deliver pre-ordered for delivery orders go out. And then, um, we also try to make

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:44.180
 extra of everything. And we have a brick and mortar store during our pickup hours. So during pickup hours,

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:49.570
 you can pick up and you can add something on. Also, you can just walk in off the street and,

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:56.360
 um, and purchase. Um, so in terms of what people order and how it fits into their lives,

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:02.200
 it's really the whole range. You have some people for whom this is a major component of their family's

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:12.090
 food. Um, we don't make meals. We make components, which we, uh, pack into returnable mason jars and

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:18.010
 people pay a deposit on the mason jar and then they bring it back. So we do the bone broths, we do soups,

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:25.370
 we do stews, we do sauces, uh, we do casseroles. Uh, we even started doing a salad. We have a salad

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:32.690
 in a jar every week. Um, we have desserts, uh, usually like a pudding or a gelatina, which is

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:40.280
 like a healthy jello. Um, some baked, you know, brownies, cakes, uh, things like that. Oatmeal,

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:46.600
 granola, a paleo kind of granola, which we call paleo crunch, um, fermented foods,

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:52.760
 sauerkraut, pickles of various kinds, pickled all, all different kinds of things, pickled sister

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:58.440
 Sarah seaweed and pickled jicama and all different kinds of pickled things. And then a whole range of

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:04.680
 beverages. I think we offer 13 different beverages every week. So, uh, kefirs, kombuchas, junes,

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:13.090
 uh, we're starting to make, uh, shrubs, um, dehydrated, crispy almonds. So a few snack foods too.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:20.040
 And so, um, one thing that I was worried about when we first started is this, I was worried that

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:26.440
 we were just sort of feeding into this convenience model and, um, reinforcing this, these ideas that so

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:30.040
 many Americans have about how they don't have time to cook and they just want convenience.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:34.440
 Um, but the truth is we're actually pretty inconvenient. So we don't actually,

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:39.240
 I don't think reinforce that, um, people will have to go out of their way to get to us because we have

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:44.520
 very, very limited hours and, you know, people have to go and pre-order. Um, and they don't,

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:49.400
 that you can't, I mean, there's all these ordering systems now, food service things where you can go

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:53.480
 and like, it's very convenient. You've got an app and you like, and then they deliver it to your door,

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:58.730
 like 45 minutes later. We're not at all like that. Like it's slow food. We're, we're, you know,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:05.820
 all of our pork is brined before cooking because based on some research about, um, pork's effect on

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:11.340
 the blood. And so like, you know, all of our brown rice is soaked, all of our pork is brined, all of,

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:18.150
 um, you know, we're, we're doing, everything has to be really planned in advance. And so pre-orders

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:22.630
 really help us because they help us to figure out how much of something we need to make. So we have a

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:27.910
 whole weekly production schedule that is trying to meet this, these orders. Um, the other reason that it

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:32.870
 doesn't really play into the convenience thing is that we're not, like I said, offering meals. Um,

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:39.270
 you, you still have to do something with the food. Um, we even, some of it isn't even cooked. Um,

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:43.190
 I didn't mention, but we make like a meat patty or meatloaf every week and that's frozen raw.

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:47.990
 So you have to cook that. So I think it actually encourages people to cook because they have some

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:52.500
 part of it done for them. And then they're working around it. If they bought a casserole, they make a

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:58.820
 salad. If, um, uh, our meaty pints are often like, um, like Tex-Mex ground beef would be an

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:02.660
 example. And we add liver to that. So we're adding liver to all of our ground meat dishes

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:08.790
 to, to enhance nutrition. Um, and there you're going to like get some tortillas and some lettuce

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:14.860
 and make it into, you know, it's going to be part of your taco dinner or, um, uh, you know,

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:19.980
 making, putting something over rice or, um, over pasta or whatever. So you're actually,

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:24.460
 it's giving you some components that get you partway there to your meal, but you're still cooking.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:29.310
 And so I think if, if anything, it actually encourages people to not eat out. It ends up

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:34.510
 replacing eating out for people and gets people eating at home more and cooking some components.

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:39.310
 And that's how I use it. I mean, I buy our food almost every week and I always want to have our

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:45.230
 bone broths on hand, you know, then I can make a risotto or, you know, deglaze for a sauce or something

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:49.870
 like that. We'll have our meat patties for breakfast, you know, sausage patties for breakfast

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:55.990
 or for dinner. Um, my partner will take our soups for lunch, things like that.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:23:02.820
 Yeah. That's what I was going to ask. You know, I, I recently was interviewing Anna LaPay and she was

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:09.610
 talking about that, you know, that statistic now that Americans eat more than half of their meals out

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:16.200
 of the house. And after talking to her, I was like, I wonder what that means. Like, does it mean that you

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:21.000
 go buy a rotisserie chicken at the grocery store and then you make a salad at home? I was thinking about

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:28.220
 all these different ways that, you know, I and lots of other people interact with what eating out actually

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:33.710
 is. But this seems kind of like the community supported kitchen is kind of like a systemic challenge to

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:39.690
 what eating out could be because there's help with some of these things, right? Where you don't get

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:44.410
 to just bring it home. And I mean, I guess you could just eat your meaty pint, like with the spoon,

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:51.060
 if you just heated it up or something, but it's not a whole meal, right? So it, it actually invites,

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:54.010
 it invites you into your kitchen in a certain way.

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:59.320
 Yeah, I mean, that's been my experience. That's what I've seen. I think it invites people back into

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:08.210
 their kitchen because it helps. And it, it, you know, it's sort of halfway between having a private

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:14.290
 personal chef that comes into your house and makes food just for you. And going to Whole Foods and

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:22.080
 getting, you know, whatever carry out from their deli there is. But it does, it's, it, it offers

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:28.360
 components that then people do things with, I mean, people take our soups and stews and add other

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:34.430
 vegetables to them and stretch them. Um, I mean, I, sometimes if we're not too busy in line, we'll ask

00:24:34.430 --> 00:24:38.650
 people like, what do you do with these things? Or, you know, somebody will, if somebody mentions like,

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:43.290
 um, you know, I always get, you know, do you have a pesto? I always get your pesto. And I'm like,

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:47.850
 oh, you know, I'll see. And if I can find them one, um, I'm like, well, why do you always get the

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:51.450
 pesto? What do you do with it? Because that's one of the things we make on a, on a pretty regular

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:57.270
 basis each week. And, um, and I'm just always curious about their answers. Like, um, as somebody

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:01.750
 said, oh, I'll, I, oh, I cook soba noodles and I toss it with soba noodles and that's like one meal,

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.950
 you know? And we're like, oh, really? That's so interesting. Like I wouldn't think soba noodles

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:13.190
 and a pesto, but, um, that's her thing. And, um, so no, people are interacting with the food in,

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:18.980
 in a way I feel like it really more than anything offers support. That's an, that's an in-between kind

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:24.390
 of support. Um, nothing that we're selling is coming out hot. You know, it's not like

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:28.790
 ready to eat food with the exception we do a broth bar. And I guess the other exception would be the

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:34.040
 salad in the jar, but even the salad in a jar, you know, we recommend people take it out of the fridge

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:42.490
 15 minutes before, and then, you know, um, either shake it or toss it. Um, so it's, nothing's intended

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:46.420
 to be just like you open the jar and you eat it. Like everything you have to interact with

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:52.110
 in some way. And we also sell doughs, which you're actually, or, you know, DIY slash dough

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:57.390
 things. So falafel dough is one of our favorites. And so you have to cook that and you can, you know,

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:02.990
 that's, it's meant to be cooked. We also do a West African fritter dough, um, uh, like a veggie burger

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:09.950
 dough on an Indian Vata dough. So a lot of the things are like, you know, things you wouldn't,

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:14.670
 you're probably not going to make falafel from scratch, but if you've got a falafel dough,

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:21.070
 then falafel becomes a meal. So I do think that overall it has the effect of inviting people back

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:28.110
 into the kitchen and just doing, you know, some part of it and, and especially doing the sort of

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:33.570
 most nutrient dense part. So then they can fill it out with the easier stuff like a salad or like rice

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:38.370
 or like pasta or something like that. And, you know, I mean, people are going to eat pasta who can eat

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:42.930
 pasta or going to eat pasta, but how much better if you're putting like our ragu bolognese on it,

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:49.110
 which has liver in it and, you know, is grass fed beef. And, you know, I mean, whole foods though,

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:53.830
 people have this impression that whole foods quality is high. Like they're not using organic ingredients

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:59.750
 and we are, you know, so it's a whole nother level of, uh, nutrient density that we're offering.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:07.010
 Yeah. And I think that, I think that's offers this opportunity, like for transformation of what

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:14.270
 you think you can do in the kitchen too, right? There's a lot more, um, there's a lot more available

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:18.750
 to you in a, in a short amount of time between juggling all the rest of the things that go on in

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:24.490
 your life. Another way that I've heard of lots of people eating the food from the kitchen here is,

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:32.460
 um, when they're sick, right? And so using it as a big support to get these, you know,

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:38.170
 using this traditional diet model as this place to get things that they don't have time,

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:42.820
 don't have energy for, or just they don't have the capacity for. So it provides this

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:46.480
 kind of incredible service, I think, to lots of people who

00:27:48.230 --> 00:27:53.220
 don't have access to that some other way. Has that been your experience?

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:57.700
 Yeah, absolutely. I mean, a lot of people come to us because they're referred by healthcare

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:02.980
 practitioners. I would say that's just a huge, probably a third or half of our customer base.

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:09.520
 And so, yeah, they're coming here specifically for medicine, you know, in the form of food. And, um,

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:13.680
 and then you also have people who just come when they have a cold. I mean, I know people who are

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:19.340
 themselves personal chefs and yet, and they're always kind of ashamed to come here and they come,

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:23.920
 but they, they're like, I'm sick and I need some good food and I can't cook it myself.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:28.320
 You know? So from that kind of just like, you've got a cold and you want to get some of our chicken

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:34.520
 soup to, um, no, you're, you know, you're dealing with a serious health crisis. Um, you've just gotten

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:39.880
 a diagnosis of something and you need this food. And then people, a lot of people with, with chronic

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:47.320
 conditions, people who are, um, dealing with some kind of autoimmune, uh, disease and, you know,

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:54.760
 are trying to eat like a very clean sort of paleo diet or whatever. It's like, um, that's,

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:59.320
 you know, it's, that's hugely challenging. It's hugely challenging to meet those needs. And then

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:05.150
 you've got families where you've got a couple where one person is, you know, needing this special diet

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:11.080
 and the other person doesn't. And it, it really helps to be able to purchase some things that

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:18.020
 can keep you at home and, and, and sharing food. So, um, yeah, that's huge. And then the other thing

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:25.110
 is, um, people with babies and, you know, new families is another huge, huge part of our, um,

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:30.550
 customer base because these were times traditionally where you would have, you know, people would bring

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:36.850
 food and people still do, um, to some extent, but it's been interesting to watch families kind of go

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:43.880
 through these, um, ebbs and flows where, um, you know, they'll have a newborn and it'll come to us

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:47.560
 every week and buy a lot of food. And then as the kid gets a little older and they're able there,

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:51.960
 you know, the time is freed up a little bit to cook more food at home, then they'll come less

00:29:51.960 --> 00:29:56.040
 often or they'll buy fewer things and then they'll get pregnant again and have another baby and then

00:29:56.040 --> 00:30:01.800
 they'll be back again. So you see that ebb and flow happen to some extent, or even just in different

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:06.230
 circumstances. We have one family that's been shopping with us for many years. Their girls are

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:13.590
 both older. One's a first grader, one's a fourth grader now, um, or yeah, second and fourth. And, um,

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:21.930
 but, uh, the mom has just been really busy with a huge project. And so they're in like every week

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:26.930
 buying a lot of food just because that's the circumstances. And so to have, to have something

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:35.070
 that's, that's there when you need it, you know, um, I think is really, it's really a service to

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:39.390
 people, you know, because you might not need it all the time, but when you have a cold, maybe you need

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:44.710
 it or you broke your foot and you literally can't cook and you need bone broth, you know, to help you

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:49.790
 heal. It's like, if you live anywhere else, what are you going to do? You know, you can do a lot of

00:30:49.790 --> 00:30:54.310
 online ordering, you know, and get from a lot of different places with a lot of, um, packaging and a lot

00:30:54.310 --> 00:31:00.790
 of plastic, but here, you know, we keep all of that as minimal as we can and, you know, build it in.

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:08.980
 So, yeah, I see that too. Well, recently I make bone broth at home, but in the summer I make less

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:14.180
 bone broth cause it's hot. Right. And, uh, I've had to have a lot of bone broth recently. I drove like

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:20.820
 an hour out of my way to go get my bone broth, which is not as good as mine. But, um, I was thinking

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:27.890
 about that and I was thinking about how much I rely on these relationships that, uh, whether it be at

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:33.110
 the farmer's market or through farmers or just, you know, through the person that I get milk from or all

00:31:33.110 --> 00:31:41.980
 of these different places. And they feel they're flexible. Sometimes they go on vacation and then I

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:46.940
 don't get milk for a couple of weeks or it changes how my pickup works or things like that. And there's

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:54.670
 something, um, that also feels like I get to participate in an ecosystem in this way that,

00:31:54.670 --> 00:32:04.820
 um, that's flexible and has to adapt, but that, um, you know, maybe that's what living in community

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:11.190
 and eating in community actually looks like now. It felt strange to drive an hour to go get the thing

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:17.140
 that I normally cook at home, but I was so happy somebody was there. Right. Yeah. Um, I mean,

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:23.720
 I think, and that's part of the shift too, with the CSK of, um, of, of having our weekly menu change

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:30.140
 and part of how it also doesn't play into the convenience mentality because you can't, you know,

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:35.150
 you can't just get what you want when you want it. And that's so much part of our expectation around

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:41.720
 our food system. And in every instance that our food system has met that expectation, it does so at

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:47.940
 the expense of the ecology or the economy or the animals or whatever. I mean, McDonald's is the

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:56.190
 perfect example of that. And so we, we don't do that. We don't offer what, whatever you want when

00:32:56.190 --> 00:33:02.000
 you want it. You know, we offer a limited list of things for a limited period of time and a limited

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:09.000
 pickup. And so, yeah, that, um, people do have to interact with their, you know, for a food system to

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:20.900
 be, uh, sustainable and, um, and sustaining in that way. It, it, it needs to have participation on a lot

00:33:20.900 --> 00:33:26.000
 of levels. And so I really do feel like our, you know, quote unquote customers, it's, I don't really

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:31.870
 feel like they're customers, but you know, our, our supporters, they have to be active participants in

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:36.870
 this as well. I mean, even including they have to wash their own jars and count them and return them

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:43.630
 and sort them. And that's, that's built in. That's part of their, the work that they do, um, to help

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:49.400
 make this possible. So it really is a community effort. Well, so another thing that stood out for

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:56.260
 me being a volunteer here was it was the first like women run kitchen I'd ever worked in and the,

00:33:56.720 --> 00:34:05.480
 it was safe, right? It was actually pretty quiet a lot of times, but all of those like, um, sex jokes

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:10.180
 and all the things that I was really used to about working and the speed, I guess, too, like this

00:34:10.180 --> 00:34:17.320
 fairly dangerous speed that I sort of connotated with kitchen work. It was different here. There

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:21.400
 were different choices being made and they were not only being made, but they were being enforced by

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:28.090
 the whole community. So I thought, I felt kind of excited to come talk to you about how do you guys

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:33.990
 see this whole structure? I mean, we've talked about who your supporters are. We've talked a little bit

00:34:33.990 --> 00:34:40.590
 about what these choices and what you make are, but how do you run a community supported kitchen over

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:46.610
 time? And what is, what are those choices? Yeah. So that's huge. I mean, that's what I'm really,

00:34:46.810 --> 00:34:54.040
 I'm really engaged with the most at the moment is, is how we organize and, um, and how we self

00:34:54.040 --> 00:35:01.400
 organize and how we distribute, um, authority throughout the company decisions about things. Um,

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:11.590
 I think these are just extremely important and, um, complex questions, uh, around organization.

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:18.720
 And I've been very, because I'm so interested in traditional diets and have this very strong

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:26.630
 belief that traditional peoples had, you know, pre or non-industrial, um, cultures had come up with

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:36.840
 amazingly, um, um, sophisticated and wise ways to nourish themselves. I also feel like, um, many of

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:42.640
 those cultures came up with amazingly sophisticated and wise ways to organize, um, and to get things

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:50.120
 done. And I have been intrigued by those for years. And for us, we, you know, we incorporated as a

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:54.580
 cooperative. So we said at the beginning, we're going to be a worker cooperative, which essentially

00:35:54.580 --> 00:36:02.910
 means that the, the, there's no absentee owners, anybody who's an owner in the business actually

00:36:02.910 --> 00:36:08.380
 works here and they have to work here full time, 30 hours a week or more, and that the ownership is

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:13.860
 shared. And so the profits are shared among that ownership. And we currently have 20 worker owners.

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:23.650
 Um, but a lot of people associate that then with a sort of, um, uh, paralyzing consensus model where

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:27.590
 everybody has to agree on everything. And we were also very clear from the beginning that we didn't

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:34.770
 want that. Um, but we didn't necessarily know what would be in its place. And we had built so much

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:41.930
 trust among the co-founders that while we were just still sort of running the show, um, we, we,

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:45.710
 we largely actually did operate sort of on a consensus model. We would talk about everything

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:50.850
 until we came to agreement. And especially because the one man that was a co-founder with us,

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:57.690
 um, got ill fairly quickly in our, um, you know, after two years. So then it was basically four women

00:36:57.690 --> 00:37:05.020
 running the company for a good three or four years after that. Um, and being women, you don't want to

00:37:05.020 --> 00:37:10.980
 disagree with anybody. And so, you know, we did a lot of sort of, um, long, long meetings coming to

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:16.260
 agreement about lots of details and to the point where we were like, this is just not working anymore.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:24.750
 And even though we didn't subscribe to the idea of consensus, we were largely using it, um, as a

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:31.570
 default sort of thing. Um, so at that point we really started to seek out what would be our formal

00:37:31.570 --> 00:37:35.330
 governance structure. And also we were in the process of expanding the cooperative and inviting

00:37:35.330 --> 00:37:39.910
 new cooperative members in. And so, um, those two things really needed to go hand in hand.

00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:48.130
 And we stumbled upon, um, governance system called holacracy and we began to implement it and we're

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:57.110
 still using it. Um, which is, it's, it's, um, it's fairly similar to sociocracy, which is, um,

00:37:57.360 --> 00:38:06.700
 also called dynamic governance. And these are sort of, um, based on an idea of consent, but not

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:16.930
 consensus. And holacracy in particular is designed to be, to, to build in, um, lots of freedom for

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:25.940
 innovation and adaptability. Um, but at the same time, um, having, having other people weigh in on

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:33.250
 decisions, uh, who are in roles that might care about a decision. So, um, we've been very intrigued.

00:38:33.250 --> 00:38:37.230
 There's also a book that came out just a couple of years ago called reinventing organizations,

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:47.920
 which is purporting to sort of reveal this new sort of self-managing paradigm that many companies

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:53.410
 around the world are starting to use and how successful it can be really based on, um, allowing

00:38:53.410 --> 00:38:59.410
 a huge amount of freedom within the company being fairly decentralized, not having a structural

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:04.350
 hierarchy. So sometimes they have a hierarchy of functions, uh, which is a little bit different,

00:39:04.350 --> 00:39:10.290
 but not a hierarchy of people. And a bunch of us read that book and got very influenced by it.

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:14.730
 Um, and that's the article I just published on medium was critiquing one aspect of the book,

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:21.130
 which is that, uh, his assertion that it's new, that these, that these ways of organizing are new,

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:27.110
 because I actually see them very much in evidence in native California and in, in, um, cultures,

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:33.110
 uh, pre-contact cultures here in what is now California, uh, very, very similar sort of ways

00:39:33.110 --> 00:39:40.700
 of organizing. And I think actually the fact that those cultures thrived for thousands of years,

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:46.680
 you know, anywhere from eight to 10,000 years are, is the likely estimate of how long those cultures

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:51.650
 were here, um, actually gives us the best evidence of all that they work. Um, you know,

00:39:51.650 --> 00:39:57.790
 that that kind of, uh, uh, decentralization and, um,

00:39:57.940 --> 00:40:04.410
 it's a, it's a, it's, I think the best way to describe it is kind of egalitarian individualism.

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:10.220
 So there's, there's, there's a huge amount of freedom within it, but also a huge amount of

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:15.300
 commitment to the group. And that's what I think we haven't been good at finding that balance,

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:22.720
 like where, where organizations have been cooperative or egalitarian, they haven't allowed for the

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:34.670
 freedom. And that's, it's really, um, constraining and, um, and also, um, not agile. Um, but then

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:42.610
 where you've had lots of agility and, um, innovation, then you haven't had the egalitarianism. And then

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:46.910
 you have this, you know, incredible concentration of wealth that we're really experiencing once again,

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:53.110
 in this culture, which is, um, sort of a chronic situation for us of, of stratification and, you know,

00:40:53.110 --> 00:40:59.410
 just a few people holding onto a lot of the resources and a real lack of access on the part of a lot of

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:05.470
 people. So, um, so this is part of what we're doing here at, at three stone is really experimenting

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:11.350
 with these, uh, self-management principles and using holacracy as our governance system. And personally,

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:18.460
 I really love it. I'm learning a huge amount through that and, um, and feel like it's, it's also bringing

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:22.180
 us and, and it's, you know, it's interesting that you started out by mentioning that we're sort of

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:31.890
 women run. We are still a majority of, I think of the 20 worker owners only, um, four are men. So

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:38.830
 four or five are men, but so the majority of us are women, um, and the majority, and that's about the

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:46.060
 same for, in terms of the employee breakdown, um, maybe a quarter men here. Um, and three quarters

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:51.260
 women are two thirds, a third, something like that. So it's definitely a lot of women, but women tend

00:41:51.260 --> 00:42:00.290
 to have, um, tend to be fairly conflict averse in my experience. And, and so there's a lot of, um,

00:42:01.010 --> 00:42:10.260
 uh, lack of clarity and lack of being explicit, um, a sort of allergy to taking on authority and

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:17.050
 expressing authority. And so, um, all the things, the positive things that you commented on, you know,

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:25.480
 the, the sort of inclusivity and the nurturing aspect of the kitchen are, um, are there, but what

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:29.980
 we need to work on is the clarity, the explicitness and the willingness to take on authority and make

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:35.930
 difficult decisions and move things forward, um, without everybody agreeing necessarily, you know,

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:42.870
 so, um, holacracy is, is helpful in terms of that. I think it, it helps us to strike a balance.

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:46.340
 Um, we're still feeling our way through with that for sure.

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:53.280
 That seems like a huge challenge is to, well, one is to form a model, right? And see what that's like

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:58.140
 to start it. But then those things change over time, right? So they need, that needs to happen

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:04.920
 as people's lives change and as the size and the scale changes and, um, in order to continue to make

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:12.080
 those choices that matter to whatever the values of what you're, what you're doing are. Um, I think

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:20.210
 another challenge I see is like, is price, right? Is price and access. And I know that when I was here,

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:26.070
 that was a big conversation that was happening. I'm sure it's not over. Like what, how has that

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:34.600
 changed over the time of running this business? And yeah, so no, it's, it, it continues to be, um,

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:42.660
 just a huge question and a huge sort of, uh, in some ways unanswerable question. Um, I, you know,

00:43:42.660 --> 00:43:49.370
 I've spent a lot of time feeling really stuck between needing to pay farmers well and ranchers

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:55.530
 well. And, um, we'd actually like to pay farmers and ranchers more than we do. We'd like to buy

00:43:55.530 --> 00:44:04.220
 even, um, even cleaner food than we do. Um, and that would, you know, for what, you know,

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:08.300
 because of our economy, the cleaner the food, the more expensive, you know, in terms of ingredients

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:15.620
 for the most part. Um, and so, you know, on the one hand, really wanting to pay farmers and ranchers

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:25.060
 well, um, on another hand, so I'm going to have three hands here, um, on a second hand, also wanting

00:44:25.060 --> 00:44:32.180
 to pay our staff a living wage, which has become an extremely, um, challenging thing as the Bay Area

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:37.480
 economy has gotten so insane with the stratification that I was talking about and housing prices are

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:48.390
 through the roof. And, um, people just can't get by in the Bay Area anymore, um, on less than some

00:44:48.390 --> 00:44:54.620
 number. And, you know, there's various different numbers that are out there. Um, and so needing to

00:44:54.620 --> 00:45:01.560
 pay staff well, and at the same time, keep food prices reasonable. And that just feels like,

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:09.140
 and, you know, a huge tangle, like, how do you do it? Um, we've been really focusing as the

00:45:09.140 --> 00:45:14.340
 cooperative has grown, we've been really focusing on raising wages. And that's like the top of our list

00:45:14.340 --> 00:45:21.060
 of what we're trying to do because everybody in this company is underpaid and people can't stay

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:27.050
 and people can't live in the Bay Area. Um, and the only thing that we've been able to do is raise

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:33.930
 prices. Um, we, you know, the, the other, the dream for a long time was, um, that we could somehow

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:43.190
 create enough efficiencies that we could avoid that, but I just don't think we can. Um, and, uh,

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:48.840
 so, you know, we've been raising prices and that's depressed demand to some extent. Um,

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:57.620
 but it's also, uh, it's helped us to, um, create a more livable working situation. Um,

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:04.310
 but then the access question looms large and, uh, you know, there's a huge number of people that

00:46:04.310 --> 00:46:09.400
 don't, that wouldn't necessarily value the food that we're making. And so then that becomes an

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:16.000
 education question. Um, if we care, which we do. Um, but there are some people who value the food that

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:20.800
 we're making that feel that they can't afford it. And that gets really tricky too, because,

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:27.670
 um, you know, some people actually can't afford it and some people just feel that they can't afford

00:46:27.670 --> 00:46:34.690
 it. And that's, that's a really, you know, it's, that's a really tough call. I mean, it, um,

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:42.620
 thinking that, you know, there, there are people for whom, you know, other things just become more

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:49.710
 valuable. And, um, we have a society also that's pressuring us a huge amount around technology and

00:46:49.710 --> 00:46:57.350
 other things. And so, um, you know, we've readily all taken on, you know, cell phone contracts that

00:46:57.350 --> 00:47:03.860
 are, if we took that money and we spent it on pastured meats instead, it's a pretty big chunk of

00:47:03.860 --> 00:47:09.200
 budget. Um, and also as we're spending, so, you know, as a society, we're spending less and less on

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:15.730
 food as a percentage every year. So even though people have this perception that food prices are

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:21.110
 going up and up and up, um, I think the figures are something like around 1970, we were spending

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:29.370
 about 18%, 16 or 18% of income on food. Now we're spending about 9%. Um, also then we were spending

00:47:29.370 --> 00:47:38.310
 about 9% of income on healthcare and now we're spending like 16, 17%. So we're spending much more

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:43.450
 on healthcare and much less on food. And I think those two are directly related. Um, but not

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:49.050
 everybody sees it that way. Um, and so we have this perception that this food is so expensive

00:47:49.050 --> 00:47:55.750
 and yet, um, you know, in, in parts of Africa, people are spending 40% of income on food and

00:47:55.750 --> 00:48:02.060
 we're spending nine. And so, uh, partly it has to do with, um, how much we're spending on housing

00:48:02.060 --> 00:48:09.420
 and how much we're spending on everything else, um, that we think is, you know, uh, a necessity of,

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:13.140
 you know, for a long time, I've been frustrated with what we consider necessities versus what we

00:48:13.140 --> 00:48:17.600
 consider luxuries. And people think of this food as a luxury, but I think of it as a necessity.

00:48:17.860 --> 00:48:21.900
 And I think there's a lot of other things that are actually luxuries like cell phones,

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:28.470
 you know, so, so that's tough. And yet at the same time, there are, you know, there are huge

00:48:28.470 --> 00:48:36.730
 problems of, um, wealth concentration. And there are people who, um, are living, living in extremely

00:48:36.730 --> 00:48:44.580
 limited economic, uh, situations. And, and, and then with, you know, what I consider like a real

00:48:44.580 --> 00:48:50.150
 health crisis going on too, we see more and more people that like literally can't work because they're

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:54.700
 too ill to work because they're dealing with some major health crisis and they need this food more

00:48:54.700 --> 00:49:02.110
 than anybody. Um, but they have no money and, uh, they may have the education whereby they could be

00:49:02.110 --> 00:49:12.180
 earning a good living, but, um, you know, through, uh, some kind of autoimmune or, um, Lyme disease or

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:19.700
 something like they're severely debilitated. And so, uh, we definitely see those folks and that's really

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:25.130
 heartbreaking. Um, so yeah, it's, it's, it's very complex. We've got some things that we're

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:31.850
 working on, um, to try to move out of that scrunch that we feel like we're in, uh, because

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:37.620
 it's really, um, it's something that we have to figure out for the model. If the model is going to

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:45.760
 persist, um, if it's going to thrive and it's going to truly do what it aims to do, uh, we somehow have

00:49:45.760 --> 00:49:54.020
 to figure it out in the context of this economic system, which, um, you know, subsidizes, uh, toxic

00:49:54.020 --> 00:50:02.740
 food and, um, makes, uh, true food, you know, nourishing food really expensive.

00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:13.430
 Well, so maybe let's, let's wrap it up by, I'm just curious, well, this thing that keeps up coming

00:50:13.430 --> 00:50:19.430
 up this season for me is with these unseen stories of food or that there's just a lot of unanswered

00:50:19.430 --> 00:50:24.930
 questions, right? So when you work on something like transformation, transformation is not known,

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:35.870
 right? Transformation is transforming. And what, like, why do that? You know, why, why work on the

00:50:35.870 --> 00:50:42.100
 system? Why create a model? Why do this thing where, you know, there are going to be these questions that

00:50:42.100 --> 00:50:49.160
 are unanswered. What, what happens as you push this thing forward? That might be more than one

00:50:49.160 --> 00:50:55.180
 question. Yeah. Um, I mean, really, this is the question of life. Like why, why do anything? Um,

00:50:55.690 --> 00:51:05.430
 I mean, to me, uh, I love all this because it's real. And, um, you know, the question of how does a

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:11.610
 community nourish itself in a way that is sustaining and sustainable is a timeless question that human

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:19.120
 communities have grappled with forever. And we study, we go and we dig up bones and we analyze them to try

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:26.430
 to figure out how some group answered this question in some place in some time. Um, and so I feel like

00:51:26.430 --> 00:51:35.300
 my day-to-day work is engaged with a very central and timeless question of being a human being on the

00:51:35.300 --> 00:51:46.970
 planet. Um, how we organize, how we make decisions, um, uh, all of this is, uh, totally universal and

00:51:46.970 --> 00:51:53.060
 completely connected to with food. I mean, I'm very interested in sort of like anthropological

00:51:53.060 --> 00:52:01.190
 research, for example, that looks at hunter-gatherer type food strategies and sees that there's certain

00:52:01.190 --> 00:52:07.690
 organizational, um, um, aspects that go along with it. Um, you, you know, in a hunter-gatherer

00:52:07.690 --> 00:52:12.830
 situation, there is absolutely no motivation to accumulate wealth because anything that you have,

00:52:12.890 --> 00:52:19.580
 you have to carry. And so you don't want to carry too much. And so, um, you know, the, the distinction

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:25.040
 between sort of a quote unquote hunter-gatherer approach versus an agriculturalist approach is

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:29.790
 very interesting to me. And then, you know, what, one, part of what I find intriguing about permaculture

00:52:29.790 --> 00:52:34.050
 is that it's kind of trying to draw from both of those and to put them together in some way.

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:42.670
 And I think that you could very much describe, uh, pre-contact California indigenous food strategies

00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:49.110
 as being permacultural. And, um, uh, there's a great book that everybody should read called Tending

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:53.530
 the Wild written by MCAT Anderson, which is the other book I referenced in my article on Medium

00:52:53.530 --> 00:53:02.730
 that is, um, basically chronicling how the communities that were here before European, uh,

00:53:03.440 --> 00:53:10.910
 decimation of them, um, how they manage their food system. And it's extremely permacultural,

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:17.270
 you know, it's like presciently permacultural. Um, so we're kind of like stumbling on this permaculture

00:53:17.270 --> 00:53:21.290
 thing, like, oh, isn't this a great idea? Like, let's make it a little bit less work and less disruptive

00:53:21.290 --> 00:53:29.520
 to the system than agriculture, but still active and participatory and, um, uh, and, and sort of

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:33.940
 looking at these zones and all this kind of stuff. Well, the indigenous people here were doing this for

00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:42.920
 8,000 years. And so, which a is great, great feedback that actually that could work and does work. Um,

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:46.900
 of course they weren't dealing with the population density that we're dealing with. And so that adds

00:53:46.900 --> 00:53:51.880
 a whole nother factor. Um, but these things are real, these, these are real questions. So yeah,

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:56.580
 I think part of the thing about the transformation is it's open-ended. We don't, you know, we have,

